                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "237228033",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561197974162681",
                            "num_reviews": 16,
                            "playtime_forever": 9023,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 7481,
                            "last_played": 1791926930
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I recommend this game, yes, but with heavy caveats. I'll just list my concerns. All of that to be said ... there are some good bones to the game here, once and if you move past the problems. Maybe the \"definitive\" edition update will help some.\n\nIt is difficult for me to call this a sim. It is very close, and there are some really neat things that I enjoyed seeing as somebody who has actually done some amateur gold mining. But... the harsh reality is that the voxel system that is setup here leads to easy and illogical deletion of gold and overburden/junk dirt... and it is not very simulation like at all in that regard. Miners can rely on one thing in the real world ... if there is gold in the dirt, it won't disappear if that dirt is moved somewhere else on the ground. But you just cannot make that same assumption in this game, leading to penalizing players for doing things that actual miners do. Heh, it also penalizes players for doing things miners shouldn't do particularly in regards to overextension, buckets, and load, but not in ways that make sense. \n\nAlso, while I was a fan of the game kindly preventing you from pushing full mats into a full bucket, I felt gut kicked when I discovered the tier 6 mats not to have the same failsafe all of the sudden. Imagine that moment when you realize you probably just deleted 2000+ oz of gold, in a scenario where you simply would resume washing the mat in a cleaner bucket once you realized your error. (Especially remembering that we aren't allowed to use the mason tub for this task, which is what we actually do... 5 gallon buckets are tiny for anything bigger than a gold hog's mats.)",
